```markdown
---
title: "Geospatial Nearby Search"
category: "Social & Discovery"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["geospatial", "geohash", "quadtree", "redis", "kafka", "location-updates", "nearest-neighbor"]
---

## Overview

This system answers “what’s near me?” for two very different shapes of data: mostly-static points of interest (restaurants) and highly-dynamic entities (drivers). The elegant trick is to **separate “truth” from “index”**: store the authoritative, latest location in a fast key-value store, and maintain a deliberately *lossy, approximate* geospatial index (Geohash/Quadtree buckets) used only to generate candidates.

Naive designs die by a thousand cuts: every location update forces deletes/inserts in multiple spatial buckets, shards get hot around downtown, and queries either scan too much (slow) or trust the index too much (wrong). This design makes updates cheap by letting the spatial index be **eventually consistent with bounded staleness**, and makes queries correct by always validating candidates against the authoritative “latest location” record.

## What Makes This Hard

The trap is treating the spatial index as the source of truth. With frequent updates, a “clean” index requires removing each entity from its previous cell on every update. At scale, that becomes (1) write amplification, (2) contention on hot cells, and (3) correctness bugs when deletes race inserts.

The non-obvious insight: **you don’t need a perfectly maintained index** to serve correct nearby results. You need a fast way to produce *likely candidates*, and a fast way to verify *the latest* position. Once you embrace that, you can tolerate stale index entries and clean them up lazily.

## Requirements

### Functional Requirements
- Nearby search by lat/lon + radius, returning top K results by distance (optionally filtered by type, availability, and freshness).
- Frequent location updates for moving entities; each entity has exactly one “current” location.
- Results must exclude entities with stale locations (e.g., driver last update > N seconds).
- Support both static POIs (rare updates) and dynamic entities (continuous updates) with the same query path.

### Scale Targets
- **Dynamic entities:** 1M active drivers, median update every 3s ⇒ ~330k updates/sec peak in bursts (events end, rush hour).
- **Queries:** 50k QPS peak, p95 latency **< 50ms** (users abandon “nearby” UIs quickly).
- **Search radius:** typical 0.5–5km; worst-case 50km (fallback / sparse areas).
These numbers matter because they force: cheap writes, hot-spot resistant bucketing, and low-latency candidate verification.

## Key Design Decisions

- **Truth in Redis Hash, index in Redis Sets**
  - Chose: authoritative per-entity location record in Redis (`entity_id -> {lat, lon, ts, version, attrs}`) + geohash/quadtree buckets as candidate lists.
  - Rejected: treating the geospatial index as authoritative.
  - Why: correctness comes from reading one key per candidate; index can be stale without breaking results.

- **Append-only bucket entries with TTL + versioned validation**
  - Chose: on update, add the entity to the *new* cell bucket and do not synchronously remove it from the old one; buckets expire quickly.
  - Rejected: per-update “move” transactions that remove from old bucket every time.
  - Why: eliminates delete amplification and write contention; query-time validation makes staleness harmless.

- **Dynamic precision (cell size) driven by radius**
  - Chose: map query radius to geohash precision (or quadtree depth) so candidate counts stay bounded.
  - Rejected: fixed precision for all queries.
  - Why: fixed precision either explodes candidates for large radius or misses results for small radius due to boundary effects.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  C[Client] --> G[API Gateway]
  G --> Q[Nearby Search]
  C --> I[Location Ingest]
  I --> K[Kafka]
  K --> U[Location Updater]
  U --> L[Redis: Latest Loc]
  U --> B[Redis: Geo Buckets]
  Q --> L
  Q --> B
  Q --> P[(Postgres: POI Data)]
```

### Components

- `Location Ingest`
  - Accepts location updates, authenticates entity, normalizes payload, applies coarse rate limits (per entity), and emits to Kafka.
  - Earns its place because it protects the rest of the system from bursty mobile clients and retries.

- `Kafka`
  - Buffers spikes and provides ordered streams per entity (partition by `entity_id`).
  - This lets you size the updater fleet for average throughput while still absorbing peak events.

- `Location Updater`
  - Consumes updates, writes the authoritative latest location record, and appends the entity into the appropriate geohash/quadtree bucket(s) with TTL.
  - The only writer of “truth,” keeping semantics clean.

- `Redis: Latest Loc`
  - Stores `lat/lon`, `updated_at`, `version` (monotonic per entity), and minimal query-time filter attributes (e.g., availability).
  - Enables fast validation and distance calculation at query time.

- `Redis: Geo Buckets`
  - Buckets keyed by geohash prefix (or quadtree cell id). Values are a set/zset of `entity_id` (optionally scored by `updated_at`).
  - Designed to be stale-safe: old memberships expire; queries verify against `Latest Loc`.

- `Nearby Search`
  - Computes covering cells for (center, radius), fetches candidate IDs, reads current locations, filters by freshness and exact distance, ranks and returns top K.
  - Owns the correctness contract.

- `Postgres: POI Data`
  - Stores business metadata (name, category, hours). Static and strongly consistent.
  - Kept out of the hot path except for enriching the final top K.

## Deep Dive: Frequent Updates Without Index Thrash

The hardest part is supporting hundreds of thousands of updates per second without turning your spatial index into a write-amplification machine.

### Data model (Geohash version; Quadtree is analogous)
- **Latest location (truth):**
  - Key: `loc:{entity_id}`
  - Value: `{lat, lon, updated_at_ms, version, attrs}`
- **Bucket membership (index):**
  - Key: `bucket:{precision}:{geohash_prefix}`
  - Value: `ZSET(entity_id -> updated_at_ms)` (or `SET` if you don’t need scoring)

### Update path (cheap and race-safe)
1. Ingest assigns/validates `version` (or the updater increments it); only the highest version wins.
2. Updater writes `loc:{entity_id}` with `(lat, lon, updated_at, version)`.
3. Updater computes `geohash_prefix` at chosen precision for that entity type (drivers often use tighter precision than POIs).
4. Updater `ZADD bucket:{p}:{cell} updated_at entity_id` and sets/extends TTL on the bucket key (e.g., 2–10 minutes depending on freshness policy).

Critically: **no synchronous removal from the old bucket**. That’s where naive designs burn CPU and create correctness races.

### Query path (correct despite stale buckets)
1. Choose precision `p` based on radius `r` (small radius ⇒ higher precision).
2. Compute the set of geohash prefixes that cover the query circle’s bounding box (and include neighbors to handle boundary misses).
3. Fetch candidates: union IDs from those buckets (cap by score recency and per-bucket limit to avoid blowups).
4. Batch `MGET`/pipeline fetch `loc:{entity_id}` for candidates.
5. Validate:
   - Drop if `updated_at` too old.
   - Drop if entity is unavailable.
   - Compute exact distance (Haversine); keep within radius.
6. Partial sort top K by distance; enrich from Postgres for POIs.

Stale membership becomes a small, bounded tax: a candidate that no longer belongs in that cell gets filtered out instantly because its authoritative location says so.

### Why this stays fast under load
- Updates are O(1): one truth write + one bucket append.
- Hot cells still get more writes, but they’re append-only and expire; you avoid delete storms.
- Queries are dominated by (a) number of covering cells and (b) candidate cap; both are tunable knobs.

## Trade-offs

| Optimized For | Sacrificed |
|--------------|------------|
| High write throughput on updates | Some query-time filtering overhead |
| Simple, correct semantics (truth vs index) | Slightly stale candidate index |
| Low latency for typical radii | Large-radius queries need stricter caps/precision |

## Failure Modes

- **Kafka lag spikes (stale truth)**
  - What happens: driver locations stop advancing; results look “frozen.”
  - Detect: consumer lag alarms + % of entities failing freshness cutoff.
  - Recover: autoscale updater consumers; temporarily tighten freshness cutoff to avoid returning stale drivers.

- **Redis memory pressure / eviction**
  - What happens: missing `loc:{entity_id}` makes candidates unverifiable; results degrade.
  - Detect: Redis `evicted_keys`, hit rate drop, latency rise.
  - Recover: reserve memory for `Latest Loc` via separate Redis cluster or logical DB; reduce bucket TTL and candidate caps first (index is expendable).

- **Downtown hot-spot cells**
  - What happens: a few bucket keys dominate traffic; latency spikes.
  - Detect: per-key command stats / sampled hot keys; uneven shard CPU.
  - Recover: increase precision in dense areas (smaller cells), and enforce per-bucket candidate limits sorted by recency.

## What I'd Do Differently At...

- **10x scale:**
  - Split Redis into two clusters: `Latest Loc` (no-evict, higher priority) and `Geo Buckets` (evictable).
  - Add adaptive precision: density-aware cell sizing so Manhattan doesn’t behave like rural areas.

- **100x scale:**
  - Replace “bucket sets in Redis” with a purpose-built geo index service using hierarchical cells (still Geohash/Quadtree conceptually) backed by sharded RocksDB, and keep Redis only for the hottest truth cache.
  - Add multi-region active-active with per-region truth and user-affinity; cross-region consistency isn’t worth the latency for nearby search.

## Operational Notes

- The two knobs that control everything are `precision(r)` and `candidate_cap`. Set them so that worst-case candidate verification fits your p95 budget.
- Treat freshness as a product feature and a safety valve: tightening `max_age_sec` is the fastest way to recover from upstream lag.
- Instrument: candidates fetched, candidates verified, candidates kept, and “stale membership rate” (bucket hit but truth in different cell). That last metric tells you when TTL/precision is wrong.
```
